A login shell that exists and is executable is a valid login shell.

dropbear validates the login shell against getusershell(), which reads
/etc/shells. On a host that has one, a passwd entry naming a shell the file does
not list is refused, and the login is denied with a message that names the user
rather than the shell:

    Login attempt with wrong user root from localhost:...
    Exit before auth from <localhost:...>: (user 'root', 0 fails)

with the user present, the shell present and the shell executable. The same
dropbear on a host with no /etc/shells works, because getusershell() then
returns a compiled-in fallback list, so whether a login succeeds depended on
whether a POLICY FILE existed.

/etc/shells is a policy for a multi-user system. A cage has one user, no
administrator and no policy, so the check's only effect there is to refuse a
shell that is present. This patch consults the list first, so a host with a
policy keeps it, and accepts an executable shell when the list does not name
one.

It is not a way around a security control: anyone who can write /etc/passwd can
already name any shell, and the list is still consulted first where one exists.
What changes is that a missing or narrow policy file stops being a login denial.

diff --git a/src/svr-auth.c b/src/svr-auth.c
index ca19382..f95031e 100644
--- a/src/svr-auth.c
+++ b/src/svr-auth.c
@@ -323,12 +323,39 @@ static int checkusername(const char *username, unsigned int userlen) {
 			goto goodshell;
 		}
 	}
-	/* no matching shell */
+	/* No match in the list.
+	 *
+	 * /etc/shells is a policy for a multi-user system: it is where an
+	 * administrator says which shells users may log in with. A cage has
+	 * one user, no administrator and no policy, and on such a host the
+	 * check's only effect is to refuse a shell that is present.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on a GitHub Actions runner, 2026-09-27, with a passwd entry
+	 * naming /bin/sh, that file present, the shell present and the shell
+	 * executable:
+	 *
+	 *   Login attempt with wrong user root from localhost:...
+	 *   Exit before auth from <localhost:...>: (user 'root', 0 fails)
+	 *
+	 * The same binary on a host with no /etc/shells works, because
+	 * getusershell() then returns a compiled-in fallback. So whether a
+	 * login succeeded depended on whether a POLICY FILE existed, which
+	 * is the opposite of what a cage wants.
+	 *
+	 * So a shell that exists and is executable is a login shell. The list
+	 * is consulted first, so a host that does have a policy keeps it, and
+	 * this is not a way around one: anyone who can write /etc/passwd can
+	 * already name any shell. What changes is that a missing or narrow
+	 * policy file stops being a login denial. */
 	endusershell();
-	TRACE(("no matching shell"))
+	if (access(usershell, X_OK) == 0) {
+		TRACE(("shell '%s' is not listed but is executable", usershell))
+		goto goodshell;
+	}
+	TRACE(("no matching shell, and '%s' is not executable", usershell))
 	ses.authstate.checkusername_failed = 1;
-	dropbear_log(LOG_WARNING, "User '%s' has invalid shell, rejected",
-				ses.authstate.pw_name);
+	dropbear_log(LOG_WARNING, "User '%s' has an invalid shell '%s', rejected",
+				ses.authstate.pw_name, usershell);
 	return DROPBEAR_FAILURE;
 	
 goodshell:
